<TopicPage slug="subjects/journalism/intermediate/the-inverted-pyramid">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The inverted pyramid is <strong>the oldest information-architecture pattern in
    professional writing</strong> — hierarchy engineered for speed (the reader's and the
    editor's). You have already met it as a shape; this page teaches it as a discipline,
    because the pyramid is really a stack of decisions: what is the single most important
    fact, what is the second, what can afford to be cut. Learning it is learning to
    decide what matters most in a story, which is the editor's core skill. The pattern
    also explains much of what news writing looks like from the outside — the short
    first sentence, the quotes bunched early, the "background" paragraph near the end —
    and mastering it is the fastest route into every other newsroom craft, from
    sub-editing to feature writing. This page covers the shape and its history, the
    anatomy paragraph by paragraph, the writing mechanics that make it work (attribution,
    active voice, honest numbers), the structures that replace it in feature writing,
    and the evidence for why it survives the mobile age.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the shape, the lede, quotes and nut graf, the alternatives" widget="equationStep" config={pyramidCfg} />

  <h2>The shape: importance in descending order</h2>
  <p>
    The pyramid turns narrative upside down. A story (in fiction, a film, an essay)
    builds: setup, development, climax, resolution — the payoff at the end. The
    inverted pyramid pays <strong>first</strong>: the conclusion (what happened) is the lede,
    the supporting evidence follows in descending importance, and the background and
    context trail at the bottom. The property that makes the shape genuinely useful
    rather than merely traditional is <strong>cut-anywhere safety</strong>: because each
    paragraph is less essential than the one above, an editor can cut the story from
    the bottom to fit any space — to 400 words, 200, 100 — and the remainder is still
    a complete, true story, just shorter. No narrative structure can do this (cut the
    last act of a play and it is broken); it is why the wire services adopted the
    pyramid and why it still governs wire copy, press releases, alerts and breaking-news
    posts. The origin story is part history, part folklore: the popular version credits
    Civil War correspondents filing by telegraph, who front-loaded in case the line
    went down; historians of journalism (Brian S. Carroll's account is the standard
    sceptical treatment) find the evidence thinner and date the pyramid's real
    consolidation to the late nineteenth century, when telegraph and then telephone
    wire services — the Associated Press above all — needed copy that dozens of papers
    could cut and print without distortion. Either way, the economics were the same:
    expensive bandwidth, unreliable transmission, many customers.
  </p>

  <h2>The lede: one sentence that decides everything</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>lede</strong> (newsroom spelling of "lead") is the story compressed to a
    sentence — conventionally under 30–35 words — and it is where most of the craft's
    discipline concentrates. A <strong>summary lede</strong> answers the reader's first
    questions: <em>who, what, when, where</em> — with <em>why</em> and <em>how</em> following
    if they fit. <strong>A worked example.</strong> The raw material: a city council,
    Tuesday night, six-hour hearing, voted 7–4 to approve a 900-home development on a
    former airfield, after the planning officer said the scheme included 270 affordable
    homes. The lede: "The council approved a 900-home development on the old airfield
    site on Tuesday, voting 7–4 after a six-hour hearing at which residents objected to
    traffic." Twenty-three words, four of the five Ws, and the conflict — the reason
    anyone cares. Notice what the lede <em>withheld</em>: the 270 affordable homes (that
    is paragraph two), the history of the site (bottom third), the quotes (supporting
    the middle). A <strong>hard lede</strong> like this suits breaking news; a <strong>soft or
    delayed lede</strong> — an anecdote, a scene, a question — suits features, and buys its
    indulgence only by delivering a <strong>nut graf</strong> within a paragraph or two: the
    paragraph that tells the reader why this story matters (the pyramid, re-entering
    through the side door). The test of a lede: if a reader stopped after it, would
    they be correctly informed? If the answer is no, the lede is decoration.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the lede is just "the first sentence", so any
  sentence will do as long as one comes first. In fact the lede is the story's
  <em>claim</em> — a specific, sourced, sequenced assertion that everything after it
  supports. The most common student failure, the "buried lede", hides the news in
  paragraph four behind throat-clearing about meetings and weather; the second most
  common, the "buried story", writes a technically-correct lede about the wrong event
  entirely (the hearing, not the housing).</p>

  <h2>The body: quotes, attribution and descending weight</h2>
  <p>
    Below the lede, the pyramid is built from paragraphs in falling order of necessity:
    the <strong>second paragraph</strong> adds the next tier of facts (the affordable-homes
    number, the vote's significance); the <strong>quotes</strong> enter early, because
    attributed voices are evidence and life at once — the interview lesson's harvest,
    spent deliberately. The mechanics are strict. <strong>Attribution</strong> is neutral:
    "said" reports; "claimed", "insisted" and "admitted" editorialise — the verb is not
    where a reporter's doubt belongs (the evidence for doubt does that job). <strong>Quotes
    are verbatim</strong> and do work no paraphrase can: voice, position, admission.
    <strong>Numbers carry their bases</strong>: "up 20%" from what baseline, over what
    period? <strong>Sentences stay short and active</strong> — one idea each, actor before
    action ("The council approved", not "Approval was granted") — not as style
    decoration but because clarity under deadline is a moral position in a craft whose
    readers include everyone. And the pyramid's bottom third holds the <strong>context and
    background</strong>: the site's history, previous votes, what happens next (a process
    paragraph is the conventional last sentence — "The plan now goes to the regional
    planning authority for final approval").
  </p>

  <h2>Sub-editing the pyramid: the cut test and the headline</h2>
  <p>
    The pyramid earns its keep in the sub-editor's hands. Cutting a story to length is
    not trimming adjectives — it is re-deriving the hierarchy: read the story, decide
    the minimum a reader must know, and rebuild the pyramid at the new height. The
    <strong>cutline test</strong> formalises it: the story must survive being cut to any
    length, because a wire story may be printed in full, in half, or as a three-line
    brief, and the truth must be the same at every length. The <strong>headline</strong>
    follows the same law as the lede one level up: state the finding, use the present
    tense ("Council approves 900 homes on airfield site"), include the specifics that
    make it findable (names, numbers), and stay honest to the story — the headline is
    the story's promise, and a headline the body cannot cash is clickbait, whatever
    the traffic metrics say. (Headlines are also written for machines now: search and
    social have made the first words of a headline disproportionately valuable, which
    makes the honesty discipline harder and more necessary — the pyramid's lede
    principle, applied to the attention economy.)
  </p>

  <h2>When the pyramid does not fit: feature structures</h2>
  <p>
    The pyramid answers the question "what happened?"; features answer "what is it
    like, and what does it mean?", and they use architectures built for reading
    pleasure without abandoning the news contract. The <strong>Wall Street Journal
    formula</strong> is the classic hybrid: open with a specific person's scene (the
    anecdotal lede), zoom to the <strong>nut graf</strong> that names the general story the
    person represents, work the body as a pyramid-plus-narrative, and close by
    returning to the opening character — the "kicker" that rounds the circle. The
    <strong>hourglass</strong> runs a pyramid down and then turns into a chronological
    narrative, suiting crime and disaster reporting where the timeline is the story.
    Pure narrative features (long-form, magazine) use story arcs but keep a version of
    the pyramid's honesty: the reader always knows what kind of piece they are in and
    what its evidence base is. The professional judgement is per-story: deadline
    pressure, reader habit and the publication's contract all pull toward the pyramid,
    and every departure must justify itself by serving the reader's question better —
    not the writer's mood.
  </p>

  <h2>Three lengths of one story, worked</h2>
  <p>
    The pyramid's true test is the editor's knife, so run the worked example to
    the end. The raw material: a ferry crashed at the harbour mouth on Tuesday
    evening; 214 people were aboard; 31 died; the coastguard said the vessel was
    overloaded by about 60 passengers and its licence had been suspended in
    January; the operator denied the overload. <strong>At 90 words</strong> the
    story is the lede plus one paragraph: the death toll, the overload claim
    attributed to the coastguard, the operator's denial attributed, the licence
    history as one clause. <strong>At 200 words</strong>, add the survivors' accounts
    (quoted, on the record), the rescue timeline (the coastguard's own numbers:
    six boats, four hours), and the operator's statement in full. <strong>At 450
    words</strong>, add the background tier: the operator's safety record (three
    previous citations), the regulation that lets a suspended licence operate
    pending appeal, and the transport ministry's response. Notice what the
    process guarantees: the 90-word version is <em>true</em> — not a summary of a
    longer truth, but the same truth at less depth — because the hierarchy was
    decided once and executed three times. That is the professional skill this
    structure trains: deciding importance first, writing second, and letting
    every shorter version inherit the same hierarchy rather than improvise one.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The scanning evidence.</strong> Eye-tracking research (the Nielsen Norman
    Group's studies, the Poynter Institute's eyetrack projects) found news readers
    scan: headlines and first paragraphs get the majority of fixations, readership
    decays steeply by paragraph, and online, many readers read little beyond the lede
    and the first quote. The pyramid is therefore not just an editing convenience but
    a match to measured reading behaviour — front-loading is reader-respect, and the
    "readers will get to the good bit later" assumption is empirically false.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The mobile twist and the live twist.</strong> Small screens cut paragraph
    readership faster still, pushing ledes even higher; live blogs inverted the pyramid
    again (newest first, a stack of tiny pyramids); and push-alert writing is the lede
    compressed to its limit — a headline with nowhere to hide. Each format change has
    re-tested the pyramid rather than retired it, which is the pattern of a genuinely
    functional design: the constraint (reader leaves early) has not changed since the
    telegraph, only its speed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> AI summarisation now produces pyramid-shaped
    digests automatically, which pushes the human value in news writing up the stack:
    the summariser can find the facts, but the judgement about what the lede <em>should
    be</em> — the claim the story makes, the fairness of its emphasis, the honesty of
    its numbers — remains the reporter's and editor's work. And the pyramid's cultural
    export continues: press releases, police statements, product announcements and
    corporate emails all borrow the shape, often badly — which makes knowing it
    properly a defensive skill as much as a productive one.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the AP Stylebook; Carroll, "The Inverted Pyramid: Origins and Limitations" in journalism-history scholarship; Poynter's writing craft resources; Nielsen Norman Group eye-tracking research; the design lesson's progressive disclosure.</p>

